NOTE ON CODE PLEADING
In the 19th century, numerous efforts at reforming pleading in American courts took hold, some more successful than others. The most notable was the Code of Civil Procedure enacted by the New York legislature in 1848, known as the “Field Code” after David Dudley Field, the leading figure in the Code’s development. Its new approach to pleading, known as “code pleading,” was tremendously influential in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, and various versions of code pleading remain the law of many states today. Its two most radical reforms were to abolish the distinction between actions at law and suits in equity and to dispense with the myriad forms of pleading defined by the thicket of writs and forms of actions under the common law. It replaced all the old forms of action with a single form of action, the “civil action.”

Code pleading required that all pleadings satisfy the following rules:

The complaint must contain “[a] statement of the facts constituting the cause of action.”

The complaint must contain “[a] demand for relief.”

The complaint’s “allegations shall be liberally construed, with a view to substantial justice between the parties.”

See N.Y. Laws 1849, ch. 379, §§ 142, 159. In this way, the Field Code self-consciously did away with the pivotal role that legal formalities had played in common law pleading. Rather than law, “facts” now held center stage, and the key question became whether the complaint alleged “facts” (i.e., rather than legal conclusions) “constituting the cause of action” (i.e., allegations that, if true, could satisfy each element of the cause of action).

But the line between “fact” and “legal conclusion” proved less obvious than one might have supposed. Are the following allegations of “facts” or are they “legal conclusions”?

(A) The plaintiff and the defendant entered into a contract.

(B) On January 7 of this year, at 114 Main Street, the defendant assaulted the plaintiff.

